Diagnostics: where Office puts its pixels

Measurement-only TRACEs.  None of them changes behaviour; every one exists
because a question about Office's rendering could not be answered by reading
Wine's source, and each answered its question in one run.

  dxgi/device.c      the swapchain's window and terms.  Office puts its whole
                     UI in a DXGI_SWAP_EFFECT_FLIP_DISCARD swapchain on a
                     *child* NetUIHWND (1438x808 for Word's frame, 373x148 for
                     the licensing dialog, 848x654 for SETLANG), not on the
                     top-level window.  Reading the source will not tell you
                     that; the app decides it.

  d3d11/texture.c    every CreateTexture2D desc, and a pixel summary of what a
                     D3D10 Map hands back.  Proved that Office's rendering
                     produces real content -- a mapped 16x16 came back with 52
                     non-zero premultiplied pixels.  It is also how the shared
                     surfaces were found: misc 0x100 is
                     D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX, not GDI_COMPATIBLE
                     (that is 0x200), and misreading it once cost an hour.

  winex11.drv/init.c what X11DRV_client_surface_present was actually asked to
                     do, and whether the blit said yes.  A child window always
                     takes the offscreen path (needs_offscreen_rendering
                     returns TRUE for anything parented below the desktop), so
                     the client X window is XComposite-redirected, reparented
                     onto a 1x1 dummy, and StretchBlt'd onto the top-level.
                     19 presents, dst (1,1)-(1439,809), full region, blt 1.

  d2d1/device.c      which branch each glyph run takes, and the alpha texture
                     dwrite produced for it.  Office shapes 260 glyph runs per
                     SETLANG startup and rasterises four of them.

  dwrite/font.c      the alpha coverage glyphrunanalysis_CreateAlphaTexture
                     actually hands back.  This is what took Wine's rasteriser
                     off the suspect list: all 78 textures Office asks for come
                     back full (nonzero 437 of 845, peak 255), so a missing
                     glyph is never a missing glyph.

Not included, because it is six lines and only useful once: printing
`resource->device` from dxgi_resource_GetSharedHandle and the device from
d3d11_device_OpenSharedResource.  That pair is what proved Office's creating and
opening devices are genuinely different objects on different threads, so the
shared-surface gap cannot be closed by handing back the same texture.

Apply with `git apply` inside wine-src when a rendering question comes back.
Do not carry it in a build you are measuring timings with: the d3d11 Map
summary walks every pixel.

